<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aulus Gabinius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/47-bce">47 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aulus Gabinius was a Roman politician and general. As tribune in 67 BCE he passed the Gabinian law giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> sweeping command against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, and he was consul in 58 BCE. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> he restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes">Ptolemy XII</a> to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 55 BCE. Fighting for Caesar in the civil war, he was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> in the winter of 48–47 BCE and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a> soon after.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Gabinius rose mainly thanks to the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>. As tribune of the plebs in 67 BCE he brought forward the Lex Gabinia, which gave Pompey command against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> pirates, with control of the sea and of the coasts for 50 miles inland. Two of his other measures made loans of money to foreign ambassadors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> actionable, as a check on corruption in the Senate, and ordered the Senate to give audiences to foreign envoys on fixed days each year, from 1 February to 1 March.</p><p>From 66 to 62 BCE he served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> as a legate in the last phases of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a>. In 65 BCE Pompey sent him with part of his army into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> to pressure the Parthian king Phraates III into a treaty, and he then marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and brigands. Aristobulus, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcanus-ii">Hyrcanus II</a>, the high priest and king of Judea, bribed him to support his claim to the Judean throne and later accused Gabinius and Scaurus of taking bribes.</p><p>As praetor in 61 BCE he gave games of unusual splendour. He was elected consul for 58 BCE, though not without suspicion of bribery, alongside Lucius Calpurnius Piso Caesoninus. He helped Publius Clodius Pulcher bring about the exile of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> and secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> as his province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 57 BCE he marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>, defeated the army of Alexander, nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcanus-ii">Hyrcanus II</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and reinstated Hyrcanus as high priest. He suppressed revolts and rebuilt several towns. In 55 BCE Pompey persuaded him to march to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes">Ptolemy XII Auletes</a> to his throne, which he did without the consent of the Senate, after a short campaign in which the young cavalry officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> supported him. He left troops, the Gabiniani, to protect Ptolemy. On his return he defeated Alexander near Mount Tabor, killing 10,000 of his men, and in 54 BCE handed Syria to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The equites, who as tax collectors had suffered losses in the disturbances in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, were embittered against him, and he was tried on three counts. He was acquitted of treason but found guilty of extortion, with reference to the 10,000 talents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes">Ptolemy XII</a> had paid for his restoration, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> pleaded his cause at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s wish. Gabinius went into exile and his property was confiscated. Caesar recalled him after the civil war broke out in 49 BCE. After Pharsalus he was sent to transport newly levied troops to Illyricum, was attacked by the Dalmatians and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a> with difficulty. There he held out against the Pompeian commander Marcus Octavius, and a few months later died of illness, in 48 BCE or early 47 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aulus Gabinius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the winter of 48–47 BCE they defeated a Roman army under <b>Aulus Gabinius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/delmatae">Delmatae</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Salona, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-57" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aulus Gabinius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-57">What was here in 57 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
